/-!
# Equation 52.7.0.1 at a morphism of ringed spaces, and so at a morphism of schemes

[Stacks, Tag 0A0I] is the display of Section 52.7,

```
Rf_*(ℱ^∧) = (Rf_*ℱ)^∧,
```

stated there for a morphism of *schemes* `f : X ⟶ S`, a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals
`ℐ ⊆ 𝒪_S` of finite type and a quasi-coherent sheaf `ℱ` on `X`, with `ℱ^∧` the derived
completion of `ℱ` along `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` and the right hand side the derived completion of
`Rf_*ℱ` along `ℐ`. The source justifies it by citing [Stacks, Tag 0A0G], the same assertion for
a morphism of ringed topoi.

`PushforwardCommutesCompletion.lean` of this directory proves what is available of the
ringed-site statement. This file reads it at the site of opens of a ringed space, which is the
display's own setting: a morphism of schemes is a morphism of ringed spaces, and `D(𝒪_X)` for a
scheme is the derived category of sheaves of modules on the small Zariski site of `X`.

Three things are stated, in increasing strength:

* the left hand side `Rf_*(ℱ^∧)` is derived complete along `ℐ` — for an **arbitrary** object,
  which is what makes it a candidate for the right hand side at all;
* the canonical comparison `(Rf_*ℱ)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(ℱ^∧)` between the two sides is invertible for a
  derived-complete `ℱ`;
* it is invertible for an **arbitrary** `ℱ` when the local sections of the two ideal sheaves are
  nilpotent, where both derived completion functors are produced from the data rather than
  assumed.

With the instance hypothesis discharged, the first two are proved at the source's own data,
sheaves of ideals of finite type, in `PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`, and at ideal
sheaves generated by finitely many global sections in
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.RingedSpace.toRingedSiteHom`: the morphism of ringed sites presented by a morphism of
  ringed spaces, `V ↦ f⁻¹V` on opens with `f^♯` as structure map.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.toRingedSiteHom`: the same for a morphism of schemes.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RingedSpace.isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion`: the left hand
  side of the display is derived complete along `ℐ`, for an arbitrary object of `D(𝒪_X)`.
* `Stacks.RingedSpace.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isDerivedComplete` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isDerivedComplete`:
  the display, for a derived-complete object, at a morphism of ringed spaces and at a morphism
  of schemes.
* `Stacks.RingedSpace.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_forall_isNilpotent` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_forall_isNilpotent`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0A0I]** for an arbitrary object, for two ideal sheaves whose local sections are
  nilpotent, at a morphism of ringed spaces and at a morphism of schemes. The second, being
  stated for an arbitrary object of the derived category, also gives the nilpotent case of
  **[Stacks, Tag 0AKL]**: see "Remark 52.7.4" below.

## Remark 52.7.4

[Stacks, Tag 0AKL] is the remark that closes Section 52.7. Its first four sentences are
bibliography — [lurie-thesis, Theorem 6.3.1] for a derived formal functions theorem for proper
maps, [dag12, Chapter 4] with the cross-reference to More on Algebra, Section 091N for the
affine story, and [G-R, Section 2.9] for proper base change and derived completion via (ind)
coherent modules — and carry no formalizable statement. Its fifth and last sentence does carry
one: *"An analogue of (52.7.0.1) for complexes of quasi-coherent modules can be found as
[HL-P, Theorem 6.5]"*, i.e. the comparison `Rf_*(ℱ^∧) = (Rf_*ℱ)^∧` for complexes rather than for
a single quasi-coherent sheaf.

`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_forall_isNilpotent`
gives that sentence in the nilpotent regime: its `ℱ` is an arbitrary object of `D(𝒪_X)`, so an
arbitrary complex of quasi-coherent modules is one instance of it. For derived-complete complexes
it is `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme`
`.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isFiniteType_of_isDerivedComplete` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean` at the source's own data — two sheaves of ideals
of finite type — and `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme`
`.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` at ideal sheaves generated by finitely many global
sections. An arbitrary complex, at a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of finite type and
`ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, is
`Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_idealSheafData_inverseImageIdealSheaf` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`, read off the invertibility half of
[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] at a morphism of ringed sites. At a morphism of algebraic stacks it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isAlgebraicStack`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionAlgebraicStacks.lean`.

## Implementation notes

*The ideal sheaves.* The display asks for `ℐ` quasi-coherent of finite type and for the
completion on `X` to be taken along `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`. Neither restriction is imposed here, and
the statements are correspondingly more general: `ℐ` is an arbitrary sheaf of ideals of `𝒪_S`,
and the sheaf of ideals `ℐ'` on `X` along which the source's completion is taken is any one
with `f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ) ⊆ ℐ'`, which the display's `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` satisfies. The quasi-coherence
and finite-type hypotheses enter the source only through [Stacks, Tag 099F], to produce the
derived completion functors; in most statements here that conclusion is an instance hypothesis,
exactly as it is in `PushforwardCommutesCompletion.lean`, and in the `…_of_forall_isNilpotent`
ones it is discharged from nilpotence.

*What the object is.* The display's `ℱ` is a quasi-coherent sheaf; the statements below are for
an arbitrary object `K` of `D(𝒪_X)`, which contains that case (a quasi-coherent sheaf placed in
degree zero). No quasi-coherence is used.

*Non-vacuity of the instance hypotheses.* They are the conclusion of
[Stacks, Tag 099F] on `X` and on `S`, and they are inhabited:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_forall_isNilpotent`
discharges one for any sheaf of ideals whose local sections are nilpotent,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf` for any
sheaf of ideals generated by finitely many global sections, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_zeroSubmodule` for the zero
sheaf of ideals, which is of finite type. In the `…_of_forall_isNilpotent` statements both are
discharged inside the statement, so neither is carried there.

*Related statements.* For a derived-complete `ℱ` the display holds at the source's own data, two
sheaves of ideals of finite type, in `PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`, and so in
particular at ideal sheaves generated by finitely many global sections, in
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean`; both rest on
`isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isDerivedComplete` below. For an arbitrary `ℱ` the
display holds in the regime in which nilpotence produces the completion functors
(`…_of_forall_isNilpotent`), and, at a ringed *site*, with `ℐ` generated by finitely many global
sections and `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`, which is the invertibility half of
[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] for the canonical comparison map. An arbitrary `ℱ` at a sheaf of ideals of merely
finite type, where the Koszul tower that proof runs on exists only locally, is reached by
localization on the target site in
`RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf_of_isFiniteType` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionLocalToGlobal.lean`, and read at a morphism of schemes and a
quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of finite type in
`Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_idealSheafData_inverseImageIdealSheaf` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`.

*The plumbing.* `Stacks.RingedSpace.toRingedSiteHom` is not new mathematics: its three fields
are `V ↦ f⁻¹V`, the fact that this is a morphism of sites ([Stacks, Tag 00X2], as
`StacksZariskiSite.isMorphismOfSites_opensMap`) and `f^♯` read on the small Zariski site
(`Stacks.ToposBridge.commRingStructureHom`). It is the `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.Hom` form
of `Stacks.ToposBridge.ringedToposHom`, which is the same morphism in the shape whose geometric
datum is a morphism of topoi rather than a continuous functor; the derived direct image of
`Pushforward.lean` is indexed by the former.

